1724
00:57:42,530 --> 00:57:44,829
Instead of struggling to figure it out,

1725
00:57:44,830 --> 00:57:47,669
I prefer reading the manual once and get to it at once.

1726
00:57:47,670 --> 00:57:49,199
That's what I prefer.

1727
00:57:49,200 --> 00:57:50,840
- You're an academic type.
- She's different.

1728
00:57:51,400 --> 00:57:52,940
I can tell. She's focused.

1729
00:57:53,470 --> 00:57:55,570
- Studying is all about persistence.
- True.

1730
00:57:55,640 --> 00:57:57,610
We didn't stay in one place for long.

1731
00:57:58,310 --> 00:57:59,540
- We roamed around.
- Yes.

1732
00:57:59,640 --> 00:58:01,150
- I didn't stay in one place.
- That's right.

1733
00:58:01,180 --> 00:58:03,149
- Were you the same, Jae Young?
- Yes. I couldn't focus.

1734
00:58:03,150 --> 00:58:04,280
- We couldn't focus.
- I see.

1735
00:58:04,580 --> 00:58:06,480
I sat in front of my desk.

1736
00:58:06,720 --> 00:58:08,289
I did go to the study hall all the time.

1737
00:58:08,290 --> 00:58:10,419
- Exactly. You did go there.
- Yes. Me too.

1738
00:58:10,420 --> 00:58:13,190
I went there, but I slept until 10:30pm.

1739
00:58:14,860 --> 00:58:16,489
At least you slept in the study hall.

1740
00:58:16,490 --> 00:58:18,230
I went to the study hall,

1741
00:58:18,360 --> 00:58:20,170
left my backpack there, and went out.

1742
00:58:20,430 --> 00:58:22,429
(They used a study hall in different ways.)

1743
00:58:22,430 --> 00:58:24,369
- You sat in the study hall.
- We used it freely.

1744
00:58:24,370 --> 00:58:25,770
- He slept there.
- He slept.

1745
00:58:26,100 --> 00:58:29,169
- I left my backpack in there.
- You left it there.

1746
00:58:29,170 --> 00:58:30,579
I went to an internet cafe with my friends.

1747
00:58:30,580 --> 00:58:31,680
- You went to an internet cafe.
- Yes.

1748
00:58:33,240 --> 00:58:36,780
We're on fire today.

1749
00:58:36,980 --> 00:58:38,620
- Right. Yes.
- Let's wrap it up nicely.

1750
00:58:39,280 --> 00:58:40,350
Let's pull it out.

1751
00:58:40,490 --> 00:58:41,920
I really hope we do.

1752
00:58:42,320 --> 00:58:44,359
- "Kim Da Sol."
- Da Sol.

1753
00:58:44,360 --> 00:58:46,689
"I grew up picking bracken and acorns."

1754
00:58:46,690 --> 00:58:48,890
"I'm Mount Jiri's Flying Squirrel."

1755
00:58:49,330 --> 00:58:53,029
"With the memories of vibrant nature from my childhood,"

1756
00:58:53,030 --> 00:58:56,499
"I've set up my own space in the middle of Seoul city."

1757
00:58:56,500 --> 00:58:59,600
"Will you come and support me as I take a leap forward?"

1758
00:59:01,040 --> 00:59:03,009
- Is she running a restaurant?
- Exactly.

1759
00:59:03,010 --> 00:59:05,410
- Is she cooking?
- Is she a chef?

1760
00:59:05,440 --> 00:59:07,549
- Mount Jiri's...
- If it's her own space,

1761
00:59:07,550 --> 00:59:09,310
is it a studio, then?

1762
00:59:09,580 --> 00:59:10,880
- Is this the place?
- It's on the second floor.

1763
00:59:11,150 --> 00:59:12,480
- Is it nearby?
- Hold on.

1764
00:59:12,680 --> 00:59:13,919
- It's on the second floor.
- Is it over there?

1765
00:59:13,920 --> 00:59:15,020
- Right?
- The second floor of that building?

1766
00:59:15,150 --> 00:59:17,060
- Here? Yes.
- It's this building's second floor.

1767
00:59:17,320 --> 00:59:18,520
- Here?
- Yes.

1768
00:59:18,690 --> 00:59:19,760
There's no sign.

1769
00:59:20,190 --> 00:59:21,589
- What?
- What is this place?

1770
00:59:21,590 --> 00:59:23,029
It says yakgwa over here.

1771
00:59:23,030 --> 00:59:25,059
- Did you say yakgwa?
- Yakgwa?

1772
00:59:25,060 --> 00:59:26,799
- Is it a dessert cafe?
- I think so.

1773
00:59:26,800 --> 00:59:28,070
I see. Yakgwa.

1774
00:59:29,230 --> 00:59:30,940
- Hello.
- Gosh. Hello.

1775
00:59:31,040 --> 00:59:32,499
- It's cute.
- Did you send a letter to us?

1776
00:59:32,500 --> 00:59:33,809
Yes. Nice to meet you.

1777
00:59:33,810 --> 00:59:34,810
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

1778
00:59:34,811 --> 00:59:36,009
You make yakgwa here.

1779
00:59:36,010 --> 00:59:38,040
Yes, I make hangwa, the Korean traditional confections.

1780
00:59:38,240 --> 00:59:39,480
I see.

1781
00:59:39,710 --> 00:59:41,680
I serve teas and alcoholic beverages.

1782
00:59:41,810 --> 00:59:43,920
It's a place to enjoy them casually.

1783
00:59:45,950 --> 00:59:47,119
- Goodness.
- What are those?

1784
00:59:47,120 --> 00:59:48,219
Gosh. What are those?

1785
00:59:48,220 --> 00:59:50,189
These are hangwa I made.

1786
00:59:50,190 --> 00:59:52,559
- Gosh. It looks good.
- Please try them.

1787
00:59:52,560 --> 00:59:53,860
- Gosh. They're so pretty.
- Gosh.

1788
00:59:54,030 --> 00:59:55,859
- Gosh. Yeon Shik loves them.
- They look pretty.

1789
00:59:55,860 --> 00:59:57,929
- I love them.
- This is jeungpyeon,

1790
00:59:57,930 --> 00:59:59,160
- as known as sulbbang.
- Nice.

1791
00:59:59,960 --> 01:00:01,600
It's a rice cake fermented with makgeolli.

1792
01:00:01,700 --> 01:00:04,239
This is butter made with shishito peppers.

1793
01:00:04,240 --> 01:00:06,299
This is bell pepper jam.

1794
01:00:06,300 --> 01:00:07,309
It's different.

1795
01:00:07,310 --> 01:00:08,570
- It's jeungpyeon.
- It's very unique.

1796
01:00:08,640 --> 01:00:09,910
Gosh. This is crazy.

1797
01:00:10,070 --> 01:00:12,640
- It's tomato ade. Do you want it?
- Gosh. I love it.

1798
01:00:13,410 --> 01:00:14,879
(Taking a sip right away)

1799
01:00:14,880 --> 01:00:16,549
- Thanks for the drinks and food.
- Yes.

1800
01:00:16,550 --> 01:00:18,119
- Gosh. It's delicious.
- Please enjoy.

1801
01:00:18,120 --> 01:00:19,180
It's so good.

1802
01:00:20,180 --> 01:00:22,020
- Gosh. This tea. It's so aromatic.
- It's different.

1803
01:00:22,120 --> 01:00:23,759
It's really good.

1804
01:00:23,760 --> 01:00:24,790
It's delicious.

1805
01:00:25,490 --> 01:00:26,519
(Taking a bite)

1806
01:00:26,520 --> 01:00:28,159
- I think it'll taste good.
- I'll try yakgwa.

1807
01:00:28,160 --> 01:00:29,260
Yes, yakgwa.

1808
01:00:29,890 --> 01:00:31,359
- Gosh. It's good.
- Is it?

1809
01:00:31,360 --> 01:00:32,530
- This is...
- I'm glad to hear that.

1810
01:00:33,670 --> 01:00:36,130
- It's jeungpyeon.
- It's really good. Really.

1811
01:00:36,200 --> 01:00:38,599
- Goodness. This is really tasty.
- It's good, isn't it?

1812
01:00:38,600 --> 01:00:40,570
- Yes, it's really good.
- Please try it.

1813
01:00:40,670 --> 01:00:42,640
It's crispy and chewy.

1814
01:00:43,140 --> 01:00:45,510
- Right.
- Because of the potatoes in it.

1815
01:00:45,580 --> 01:00:48,279
It's crispy because I baked it in the oven.

1816
01:00:48,280 --> 01:00:50,209
And it's chewy because it's rice cake inside.

1817
01:00:50,210 --> 01:00:51,450
- Is it rice cake?
- Yes.

1818
01:00:51,720 --> 01:00:53,350
It's neither a rice cake nor bread. Try it.

1819
01:00:53,790 --> 01:00:55,450
Is this... Wait. Why is it so soft?

1820
01:00:55,620 --> 01:00:58,219
That's... Do you know about Dubai chewy cookies?

1821
01:00:58,220 --> 01:00:59,259
- Yes.
- Dubai chewy cookies. Yes.

1822
01:00:59,260 --> 01:01:00,689
During its hype,

1823
01:01:00,690 --> 01:01:03,059
I created a version with pine nuts and bean powder.

1824
01:01:03,060 --> 01:01:04,300
It's a pine nut chewy cookie.

1825
01:01:04,400 --> 01:01:05,760
Hold on.

1826
01:01:05,830 --> 01:01:08,330
- It's a long name.
- It sounds a bit... Hold on.

1827
01:01:08,370 --> 01:01:09,399
Pine nut chewy cookies.

1828
01:01:09,400 --> 01:01:10,800
- It's tasty.
- It's really good.

1829
01:01:11,370 --> 01:01:13,739
- Yakgwa tastes good too.
- It's tasty and not too sweet.

1830
01:01:13,740 --> 01:01:14,740
You're right.

1831
01:01:14,741 --> 01:01:16,010
This is good too.

1832
01:01:16,210 --> 01:01:17,379
It's so good.

1833
01:01:17,380 --> 01:01:19,410
- Pine nut chewy cookies are good.
- It's really tasty.

1834
01:01:19,480 --> 01:01:21,780
It's tasty. It's our bestseller.

1835
01:01:22,410 --> 01:01:24,349
- It's good.
- It's tasty and isn't too sweet.

1836
01:01:24,350 --> 01:01:25,650
We heard...

1837
01:01:26,080 --> 01:01:27,389
- you were from Mount Jiri.
- Mount Jiri.

1838
01:01:27,390 --> 01:01:28,590
Yes, that's right.

1839
01:01:28,790 --> 01:01:30,789
- I'm originally from Busan.
- I see.

1840
01:01:30,790 --> 01:01:34,559
But I spent much of my childhood in Mount Jiri.

1841
01:01:34,560 --> 01:01:36,459
- I see.
- I harvested mugwort in Spring.

1842
01:01:36,460 --> 01:01:37,999
- And made rice cakes with it.
- Mugwort rice cake is tasty.

1843
01:01:38,000 --> 01:01:39,929
I picked azaleas and made hwajeon, flower pancakes.

1844
01:01:39,930 --> 01:01:40,930
You made hwajeon.

1845
01:01:40,931 --> 01:01:44,300
I spent my time picking bracken and mushrooms.

1846
01:01:44,370 --> 01:01:47,039
I was naturally exposed...

1847
01:01:47,040 --> 01:01:49,710
to ingredients that were in season.

1848
01:01:50,510 --> 01:01:53,780
That led me to make Korean desserts.

1849
01:01:53,980 --> 01:01:56,879
They're very unique. I love them.

1850
01:01:56,880 --> 01:01:58,320
They're really delicious.

1851
01:01:58,480 --> 01:02:00,390
How long have you been running this cafe?

1852
01:02:00,590 --> 01:02:02,720
It's been nine months.

1853
01:02:02,820 --> 01:02:04,219
- It hasn't been long.
- No.

1854
01:02:04,220 --> 01:02:05,560
I'm a novice owner.

1855
01:02:05,860 --> 01:02:07,759
How has it been? It's been nine months.

1856
01:02:07,760 --> 01:02:10,330
Do you have regulars? Are you in a good place?

1857
01:02:10,560 --> 01:02:12,559
I have many regulars.

1858
01:02:12,560 --> 01:02:15,470
I can't say business is booming.

1859
01:02:15,600 --> 01:02:18,139
But I make enough to get by.

1860
01:02:18,140 --> 01:02:20,099
It's not super great,

1861
01:02:20,100 --> 01:02:22,110
- but it isn't bad either.
- You're right.

1862
01:02:22,570 --> 01:02:26,410
- I love talking to my customers.
- I see.

1863
01:02:26,540 --> 01:02:28,379
They try what I made...

1864
01:02:28,380 --> 01:02:31,150
and say they like it.

1865
01:02:31,750 --> 01:02:33,690
- Do you make all of these alone?
- Yes.

1866
01:02:35,650 --> 01:02:37,319
Do you have time to make them?

1867
01:02:37,320 --> 01:02:38,619
You must be so busy.

1868
01:02:38,620 --> 01:02:41,659
Well. We're closed on Mondays and Tuesdays.

1869
01:02:41,660 --> 01:02:42,759
I come on those days to...

1870
01:02:42,760 --> 01:02:44,330
It's been a while since I heard Mondays and Tuesdays with an accent.

1871
01:02:44,760 --> 01:02:45,799
I think...

1872
01:02:45,800 --> 01:02:46,960
She's from Busan, all right.

1873
01:02:47,130 --> 01:02:49,529
- Monday and Tuesday.
- Monday and Tuesday.

1874
01:02:49,530 --> 01:02:51,200
- Sunday.
- Sunday.

1875
01:02:51,600 --> 01:02:53,270
Monday. Sunday.

1876
01:02:53,370 --> 01:02:54,370
By the way,

1877
01:02:54,370 --> 01:02:55,370
- where are you from, Jae Young?
- I'm from Seoul.

1878
01:02:55,371 --> 01:02:56,770
- You're from Seoul.
- From Seodaemun. Yes.

1879
01:02:56,970 --> 01:02:59,039
- I'm from Incheon.
- Incheon.

1880
01:02:59,040 --> 01:03:00,980
But my mom is from Gyeongsang Province.

1881
01:03:01,550 --> 01:03:03,780
So people say I do have an accent.

1882
01:03:04,020 --> 01:03:05,980
When I call my aunt,

1883
01:03:06,180 --> 01:03:08,020
I say, "Aunt."

1884
01:03:08,890 --> 01:03:10,720
My friends asked me, "Why do you say aunt like that?"

1885
01:03:11,120 --> 01:03:14,730
I thought, "Then what else could I say?"

1886
01:03:14,860 --> 01:03:16,429
How do you say "Aunt," then?

1887
01:03:16,430 --> 01:03:17,900
- Aunt. Like this.
- Aunt.

1888
01:03:18,030 --> 01:03:20,030
- Aunt.
- Do you say, "Aunt?"

1889
01:03:20,130 --> 01:03:21,199
How do you say it?

1890
01:03:21,200 --> 01:03:22,700
- Aunt.
- See?

1891
01:03:23,200 --> 01:03:24,869
- You always say it like that.
- Yes.

1892
01:03:24,870 --> 01:03:25,969
- I didn't know that was different.
- No.

1893
01:03:25,970 --> 01:03:27,639
I didn't know it had a different intonation.

1894
01:03:27,640 --> 01:03:29,110
We call her without an accent. Aunt.

1895
01:03:29,310 --> 01:03:30,839
- Aunt. Just like that.
- Aunt.

1896
01:03:30,840 --> 01:03:33,310
- It just comes out with an accent.
- Yes.

1897
01:03:33,780 --> 01:03:36,610
What did you do before opening this cafe?

1898
01:03:36,750 --> 01:03:38,120
I used to design...

1899
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:40,749
- I was a designer.
- Did you?

1900
01:03:40,750 --> 01:03:42,149
I majored in design too.

1901
01:03:42,150 --> 01:03:43,150
- I see.
- Yes.

1902
01:03:43,151 --> 01:03:45,319
By the looks of it, we can tell...

1903
01:03:45,320 --> 01:03:47,289
Maybe because you studied design,

1904
01:03:47,290 --> 01:03:48,730
this space itself...

1905
01:03:49,090 --> 01:03:51,859
- is simple yet different.
- It's pretty.

1906
01:03:51,860 --> 01:03:53,799
When I first came in here...

1907
01:03:53,800 --> 01:03:55,529
Did you see the sign...

1908
01:03:55,530 --> 01:03:57,400
- in front of this place?
- Right.

1909
01:03:57,500 --> 01:04:00,169
- It was a wooden sign.
- Yes. It was made of wood.

1910
01:04:00,170 --> 01:04:03,610
The posters hanging in the kitchen were so pretty too.

1911
01:04:03,710 --> 01:04:05,939
- And the plates are pretty too.
- That's right.

1912
01:04:05,940 --> 01:04:09,080
- Thank you.
- I'm into plates and cutlery too.

1913
01:04:09,210 --> 01:04:10,779
So I like looking at them.

1914
01:04:10,780 --> 01:04:13,219
And these plates, cutlery,

1915
01:04:13,220 --> 01:04:15,820
- and cups.
- The props here.

1916
01:04:15,890 --> 01:04:17,089
Don't you appreciate it when someone notices it?

1917
01:04:17,090 --> 01:04:19,019
- Yes. I love it so much.
- She's excited.

1918
01:04:19,020 --> 01:04:20,789
We don't talk about that stuff at all.

1919
01:04:20,790 --> 01:04:23,229
- Really.
- "I love it so much."

1920
01:04:23,230 --> 01:04:25,360
(She expressed her joy with her strong accent.)

1921
01:04:25,530 --> 01:04:28,029
- When someone notices it...
- Yes.

1922
01:04:28,030 --> 01:04:30,369
When someone notices it, you're grateful for it.

1923
01:04:30,370 --> 01:04:32,139
- She even noticed little stuff.
- When there's something like this,

1924
01:04:32,140 --> 01:04:33,269
we use our hands instead.

1925
01:04:33,270 --> 01:04:35,040
- No. Don't.
- That's what usually happens.

1926
01:04:35,410 --> 01:04:36,669
- Right? You chose them with care.
- Even...

1927
01:04:36,670 --> 01:04:38,179
- those skewers...
- Yes.

1928
01:04:38,180 --> 01:04:41,149
- have two different kinds.
- These? I see.

1929
01:04:41,150 --> 01:04:43,780
- That's so important. Yes.
- They're different.

1930
01:04:44,020 --> 01:04:45,820
Is it a mosquito coil?

1931
01:04:47,320 --> 01:04:48,349
(A mosquito coil?)

1932
01:04:48,350 --> 01:04:49,589
- Goodness.
- When a customer like him comes...

1933
01:04:49,590 --> 01:04:51,089
She denied it with her whole body.

1934
01:04:51,090 --> 01:04:53,020
- No.
- She said that wasn't it.

1935
01:04:53,590 --> 01:04:55,429
- You must be happy she's here.
- Yes.

1936
01:04:55,430 --> 01:04:56,789
- I'm so proud.
- I'm so glad.

1937
01:04:56,790 --> 01:04:58,630
I just bought these.

1938
01:05:00,130 --> 01:05:02,930
- I'm so glad.
- You just got them.

1939
01:05:03,070 --> 01:05:04,140
Congratulations.

1940
01:05:06,800 --> 01:05:08,069
For her noticing your passion.

1941
01:05:08,070 --> 01:05:09,370
(Congratulating her instead of sympathizing with her)

1942
01:05:09,710 --> 01:05:11,709
Anyway, today...

1943
01:05:11,710 --> 01:05:14,850
Is this your slowest time of the day?

1944
01:05:14,950 --> 01:05:17,979
Yes. On Saturdays, we have a book club in the morning.

1945
01:05:17,980 --> 01:05:19,949
- Here?
- Once that's over,

1946
01:05:19,950 --> 01:05:21,119
I have some time.

1947
01:05:21,120 --> 01:05:22,489
Did you have a book club today?

1948
01:05:22,490 --> 01:05:23,549
- Yes, I did.
- I see.

1949
01:05:23,550 --> 01:05:26,019
I started this book club...

1950
01:05:26,020 --> 01:05:27,890
because I didn't read at all.

1951
01:05:28,490 --> 01:05:31,730
My goal was to make an hour for me to sit and read.

1952
01:05:31,930 --> 01:05:33,970
And I gathered some people while I was at it.

1953
01:05:34,130 --> 01:05:37,270
We read while listening to rice cakes getting cooked.

1954
01:05:37,840 --> 01:05:39,769
Do you have a hobby...

1955
01:05:39,770 --> 01:05:41,710
- you do after work?
- A hobby.

1956
01:05:41,770 --> 01:05:43,439
I have a longtime hobby.

1957
01:05:43,440 --> 01:05:46,009
- I quit it because I had to work.
- What is it?

1958
01:05:46,010 --> 01:05:48,509
I did soul dancing.

1959
01:05:48,510 --> 01:05:50,409
- Soul dancing?
- Yes. Do you know it?

1960
01:05:50,410 --> 01:05:51,549
No, I've never heard of it.

1961
01:05:51,550 --> 01:05:55,689
You groove to the beat and step around.

1962
01:05:55,690 --> 01:05:58,619
- A soul dance?
- Yes. I did it for two years.

1963
01:05:58,620 --> 01:06:00,559
- It's so new to me.
- Me too.

1964
01:06:00,560 --> 01:06:02,529
- I've never heard of it.
- Can you show it to us?

1965
01:06:02,530 --> 01:06:03,830
- Me?
- Yes.

